; ======================================================================
;  gpio.pi4 - BCM2711 general-purpose I/O, in readable code
; ======================================================================
;
;  PinMode / PinAlt / DigitalWrite / DigitalRead / PinToggle / PinPull
;  over all 58 of the BCM2711's GPIO lines.
;
;  A QUICK EXAMPLE
;
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"
;
;    Procedure Main()
;      PinMode(21, #PIN_OUTPUT)
;      PinPull(20, #PULL_UP)
;      PinMode(20, #PIN_INPUT)
;
;      DigitalWrite(21, DigitalRead(20))
;    EndProcedure
;
;  ---- DEPENDENCIES ---------------------------------------------------
;
;  NONE. This file includes no other library.
;
;  It uses no delays, so it does not need RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4 -
;  and the reason that is worth stating is that on a BCM2835 or BCM2836
;  it WOULD have: the old pull mechanism required a timed wait. See the
;  pull section below.
;
;  House rule 10 (vault RULES.md, 2026-07-30): a library never
;  includes another library. The MAIN file lists what it needs, one per
;  line; a dependency is documented in a header like this one and fails
;  loudly at compile time as an undefined procedure if it is missing.
;
;  ---- WHERE THE ADDRESSES COME FROM ---------------------------------
;
;  Every number in this file is traceable. Two documents, both on disk
;  under RaspberryPi4/Reference/ :
;
;    bcm283x.dtsi / bcm2711.dtsi   - which peripheral is at which BUS
;                                    address, and the bus->ARM window
;    bcm2711-peripherals.pdf       - "BCM2711 ARM Peripherals", the
;                                    register-level authority
;
;  THE BASE ADDRESS, derived and not remembered:
;
;    bcm283x.dtsi line 107:  gpio: gpio@7e200000
;    bcm2711.dtsi lines 41-43, the soc node's ranges:
;        ranges = <0x7e000000  0x0 0xfe000000  0x01800000>, ...
;
;    so bus $7E200000 sits at ARM $FE200000, which the peripherals PDF
;    states independently in section 1.2.4 (p.6): "a peripheral
;    described in this document as being at legacy address 0x7Enn_nnnn
;    ... is visible to the ARM at 0x0_FEnn_nnnn if Low Peripheral mode
;    is enabled". Low Peripheral mode is what the Pi 4 firmware sets and
;    what pi4Hello.pi4 and anvil.pi4 have both used on this board
;    (the PL011 at $FE201000 is the same window, 0x1000 further along).
;
;  ---- .l, NOT .i. THE ONE THAT SILENTLY MISCOMPILES ------------------
;
;  ***EVERY REGISTER BELOW IS 32 BITS WIDE AND IS TOUCHED WITH PeekL /
;  PokeL.*** BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 5.2 (p.66) says it outright
;  for this block: "All accesses are assumed to be 32-bit."
;
;  On this target .i, a bare pointer, and PeekI/PokeI are EIGHT bytes -
;  they were four before the 64-bit rewrite. A PeekI of GPLEV0 would be
;  a 64-bit read spanning GPLEV0 and GPLEV1, and a PokeI of GPSET0 would
;  write GPSET1 as well, setting pins you never named. Nothing faults.
;  See the LANGUAGE FACTS section of RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4
;  for the long version; the short version is that .l is not a leftover
;  from the 32-bit days, it is the width of the silicon.
;
;  PeekL SIGN-EXTENDS into the X register (the emitter appends sxtw), so
;  a register with bit 31 set - GPLEV0 with GPIO31 high, say - comes
;  back NEGATIVE. Every read in this file goes through GpioReadReg(),
;  which masks with $FFFFFFFF so that later shifts and comparisons see a
;  plain positive 32-bit value.
;
;  ---- THE PULL MECHANISM. READ THIS BEFORE PORTING ANYTHING ---------
;
;  ***THE BCM2711 DOES NOT HAVE GPPUD OR GPPUDCLK.*** Not "has them and
;  prefers something else" - they are absent from the register map. The
;  string "GPPUD" does not occur anywhere in the BCM2711 ARM Peripherals
;  document.
;
;  Every Pi 3 example, every tutorial, and a great deal of code still in
;  circulation configures a pull with the BCM2835/BCM2836 sequence:
;  write the desired state to GPPUD ($7E200094), wait 150 cycles, write
;  a pin mask to GPPUDCLK0/1 ($7E200098/$7E20009C), wait 150 cycles,
;  clear both. That is a clock-and-latch protocol, it needs a delay
;  loop, and ON THIS PART IT DOES NOTHING USEFUL. Offsets 0x94-0x9c are
;  not in Table 63 at all. Writing there is not an error - it is a write
;  into the GPIO block's address space that changes no pull, so the pin
;  keeps whatever pull the firmware left on it and the failure looks
;  like a floating input or an unexpectedly strong one.
;
;  WHAT THE BCM2711 HAS INSTEAD is four ordinary read/write registers,
;  two bits per pin, no sequence and no delay:
;
;    Table 63, section 5.2, p.66:
;      0xe4  GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0    GPIOs 0..15
;      0xe8  GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG1    GPIOs 16..31
;      0xec  GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2    GPIOs 32..47
;      0xf0  GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG3    GPIOs 48..57
;
;    Table 90, p.74, field GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL<n> at bits [2n+1:2n]:
;      00 = No resistor is selected
;      01 = Pull up resistor is selected
;      10 = Pull down resistor is selected
;      11 = Reserved
;
;    and, from the same table's description: "Reading these registers
;    gives the current pull-state" - so PinPullGet() below is a real
;    read-back, not a shadow copy.
;
;  THIS IS THE MECHANISM IMPLEMENTED HERE: a plain read-modify-write of
;  the two bits, and nothing else.
;
;  The reset values are NOT all "no pull" and are not uniform: Table 90
;  gives GPIO0-8 a reset of 0x1 (pull up) and GPIO9-15 a reset of 0x2
;  (pull down), and the per-pin defaults after a power down are in the
;  alternate function table (Table 94), also reproduced as the "Default
;  Pull" column of Table 5 in the Pi 4 product datasheet. So a pin you
;  have not configured is NOT floating, and "it reads high" is not
;  evidence of anything until you have set the pull yourself.
;
;  ---- MEMORY ORDERING -------------------------------------------------
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 1.3, pp.6-7: the AXI system does not
;  always return read data in order, and the ARM core has no logic to
;  cope with that. Accesses to the SAME peripheral are always in order;
;  it is only switching between peripherals that can reorder. The
;  document's rule is "a memory write barrier before the first write to
;  a peripheral, a memory read barrier after the last read".
;
;  Every public procedure below is a complete little peripheral service,
;  so it takes that rule literally: a barrier on entry and a barrier
;  after its last read. The barrier is a64_barrier() - dsb sy + isb -
;  from RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def.
;
;  That is deliberately the CONSERVATIVE reading, and it is not free. If
;  you are bit-banging a protocol out of DigitalWrite in a tight loop
;  and nothing else in that loop touches another peripheral, section 1.3
;  says you do not need a barrier per access - but measure before you go
;  and write an unbarriered variant, because a dsb on an A72 is cheaper
;  than the mistake it prevents.
;
;  ---- STATUS ---------------------------------------------------------
;
;  Written 2026-08-24. Compiles for -t pi4. The emitted assembly was
;  read back and checked instruction by instruction against Table 63:
;
;    GPFSEL   movz w11, #65056, lsl #16      -> $FE200000 + (pin/10)*4
;    GPSET0   movz w12, #28  + the same movk -> $FE20001C + (pin/32)*4
;    GPCLR0   movz w12, #40                  -> $FE200028 + (pin/32)*4
;    GPLEV0   movz w11, #52                  -> $FE200034 + (pin/32)*4
;    pulls    movz w11, #228                 -> $FE2000E4 + (pin/16)*4
;
;  and the field arithmetic likewise: *3 for FSEL, *2 for the pulls, and
;  PinMode's mask emitting lsl / mvn / and / lsl / orr / str w - a
;  32-bit store, which is what a 32-bit register wants.
;
;  NOT YET HARDWARE-VERIFIED. Nothing here has driven a real pin.
;
;  RE-CHECKED 2026-08-27 AND STILL TRUE, and dated twice on purpose: an
;  undated "not yet verified" reads as present tense for ever, and six
;  headers in this directory were false for days because of exactly
;  that. The check is mechanical and repeatable in one command - nothing
;  anywhere under RaspberryPi4/ carries
;  `XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"`, so no image that has ever
;  booted contains a line of this file.
;
;  DO NOT READ THE NEIGHBOURS ACROSS. Plenty of GPIO has been driven on
;  this board and none of it went through here: uart.pi4 muxes GPIO
;  14/15 with its own PokeL, bluetooth.pi4 reads GPFSEL1/GPFSEL3 directly
;  to decide where the PL011 points, and an inverted pull encoding was
;  found and fixed in the monitor. Those prove the ADDRESSES in the
;  $FE2000xx window are right - which is worth something, since a wrong
;  base is this project's most expensive recurring mistake - and prove
;  nothing whatever about the procedures below.
;
;  Two things in particular need silicon rather than a listing:
;
;    * that PinPull() actually changes a pad. The whole argument for the
;      GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL registers over GPPUD is documentary; it wants
;      a meter on a floating pin. THE PULL ENCODING IS THE PART WITH A
;      KNOWN HISTORY: an inverted one was found on this part in other
;      code, so a readback that merely looks plausible is not evidence.
;    * the reset pull states of Table 90 (GPIO0-8 up, GPIO9-15 down),
;      read back through PinPullGet() before anything writes them.
;
;  ---- SUPERSEDED CROSS-REFERENCE -------------------------------------
;
;  This header used to point at "the 32-bit-.i truncation described in
;  the STATUS note of RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4", saying it affects the
;  backend but not this file. THAT TRUNCATION WAS FIXED ON 2026-08-24
;  and timer.pi4's note has been retired to a SUPERSEDED block; the
;  pointer is kept here so a reader who finds the old wording in the git
;  history does not go looking for a live defect.
;
;  The reason it did not affect this file is still worth stating,
;  because it is why nothing here had to change when the model moved:
;  every value in this file - pin numbers, 3- and 2-bit fields, and
;  addresses in the $FE2000xx window - fits in 32 bits with room to
;  spare, so neither model can lose anything.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The register block.
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals, Table 63 (section 5.2, p.66). The GPIO
;  register base is legacy $7E200000 -> ARM $FE200000; the offsets are
;  copied from that table verbatim and the arithmetic that turns a pin
;  number into an address is in the helpers further down, so that the
;  numbers themselves are reviewable against the table one line each.
;
;  Only the registers this library actually uses are named. The event
;  detect group - GPEDS0/1 at 0x40/0x44, GPREN 0x4c/0x50, GPFEN
;  0x58/0x5c, GPHEN 0x64/0x68, GPLEN 0x70/0x74, GPAREN 0x7c/0x80,
;  GPAFEN 0x88/0x8c - is in the same table and is deliberately LEFT OUT:
;  edge and level detection is an interrupt story, and this library has
;  no interrupt story yet. Their offsets are recorded here so the next
;  person does not have to re-derive them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ONE BOARD-SPECIFIC ADDRESS IN THIS FILE. THERE ARE NO OTHERS.
;
;  Changed 2026-08-26. There used to be six absolute BCM2711 addresses
;  here; there is now one, and the five register constants below are
;  OFFSETS INSIDE THE BLOCK, which are identical on the BCM2835,
;  BCM2836, BCM2837 and BCM2711 - only the window they sit in moves.
;
;  Renaming them to "_OFF" is not cosmetic. Had #GPSET0 merely changed
;  value from $FE20001C to $1C, any use site the conversion missed
;  would still compile and would then set a bit at address $1C for
;  ever. With the rename, a missed site does not build.
;
;  This literal stays here rather than coming from
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/soc.pi4 for the reason set out at length in
;  uart.pi4: soc.pi4 would have to become a mandatory include for
;  everything at once. soc.pi4 holds the same number with fuller
;  citations, and tools/a64/a64_socbase_check.py fails if this line
;  and that file disagree.
;
;  CITATION. gpio@7e200000 - Datasheets/pi4/bcm283x.dtsi:107, and
;  bcm2711-peripherals.pdf 5.2 Register View, p.67, "The GPIO register
;  base address is 0x7e200000". Bus $7Ennnnnn is ARM physical
;  $FEnnnnnn - bcm2711.dtsi:41.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#GPIO_BASE   = $FE200000      ; bus $7E200000, translated per soc ranges

#GPFSEL0_OFF = $00            ; function select, 10 pins per reg
#GPSET0_OFF  = $1C            ; output set,      32 pins per reg
#GPCLR0_OFF  = $28            ; output clear,    32 pins per reg
#GPLEV0_OFF  = $34            ; pin level,       32 pins per reg
#GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_OFF = $E4  ; pulls,     16 pins per reg

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pin modes - the RAW three-bit FSEL codes, not an invented enumeration.
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals, Table 64 (section 5.2, p.66), which spells
;  the encoding out for every FSEL field:
;
;      000 = input          011 = alternate function 4
;      001 = output         010 = alternate function 5
;      100 = alternate function 0
;      101 = alternate function 1
;      110 = alternate function 2
;      111 = alternate function 3
;
;  ***NOTE ALT4 AND ALT5.*** The ALT codes are NOT 4+n. ALT0..ALT3 are
;  4,5,6,7 but ALT4 is 3 and ALT5 is 2 - the table is out of order and
;  it is out of order on purpose in the silicon. Writing 8 for "ALT4"
;  or computing 4+alt selects a different peripheral onto the pad, which
;  is a fault-free way to drive the wrong signal. These constants ARE
;  the table, so a reviewer can check six lines against six lines.
;
;  Holding the raw codes in the constants is the same choice
;  RA4M1AndRelated/Lib/gpio.unor4 makes with its PmnPFS values, and for
;  the same reason: one translation step is one place to be wrong.
;
;  WHY THESE ARE DECIMAL AND NOT %000 / %100, WHICH IS HOW THE DATASHEET
;  WRITES THEM AND HOW THIS FILE WAS FIRST WRITTEN: binary literals do
;  not compile on this backend. `v = %1010` emits `adrp x11, %1010` and
;  the build dies with "A64 ASSEMBLER FATAL: unknown symbol '%1010'".
;  A64LooksNumeric() in RaspberryPi4/A64Assembler.pbi handles $, 0x and
;  decimal but has no % case, so a binary literal is classified as a
;  SYMBOL. The same gap exists in A64Numeric() next to it. It is a
;  compiler defect, reported 2026-08-24, NOT fixed here - the compiler
;  sources are held by another worker. The bit pattern from the table is
;  in the comment on every line so the review against Table 64 is still
;  a six-line-against-six-line check.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PIN_INPUT  = 0               ; 000
#PIN_OUTPUT = 1               ; 001
#PIN_ALT0   = 4               ; 100
#PIN_ALT1   = 5               ; 101
#PIN_ALT2   = 6               ; 110
#PIN_ALT3   = 7               ; 111
#PIN_ALT4   = 3               ; 011   <- not 8
#PIN_ALT5   = 2               ; 010   <- not 9

#PIN_LOW  = 0
#PIN_HIGH = 1

; Pull states. Table 90, p.74. These are the raw two-bit field values.
#PULL_NONE = 0                ; 00 - no resistor is selected
#PULL_UP   = 1                ; 01
#PULL_DOWN = 2                ; 10
; 11 is Reserved and is refused by PinPull() rather than written.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  How many GPIOs there are, and why 57 and not 53.
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 5.1 (p.65): "There are 58 General-
;  Purpose Input/Output (GPIO) lines split into three banks. Bank 0
;  contains GPIOs 0 to 27, bank 1 contains GPIOs 28 to 45, and bank 2
;  contains GPIOs 46 to 57."
;
;  Confirmed by bcm2711.dtsi line 646, which overrides the inherited
;  BCM2835 node: gpio-ranges = <&gpio 0 0 58>. The bcm283x.dtsi original
;  says 54 - that is the older part, and it is exactly the sort of stale
;  number a Pi 3 port would carry in.
;
;  Only GPIO0..27 reach the 40-pin header (Pi 4 product datasheet,
;  section 5.1, "28x user GPIO"). 28..57 are real lines on the die used
;  for on-board functions, so they are addressable here but you should
;  know what you are taking over before you drive one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#PIN_MAX   = 57
#PIN_COUNT = 58

; Counts every call that was refused for an out-of-range pin. A silent
; refusal is still a silent wrong answer at 3am; this makes it countable
; without every call site having to check a return code. Same idea as
; GpioBadPinCount() in RA4M1AndRelated/Lib/gpio.unor4.
Global gpio_bad_pins.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GpioBarrier() - the section 1.3 barrier, named once.
;
;  a64_barrier() is dsb sy + isb, from
;  RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def. Exposed as a procedure
;  so that a caller sequencing GPIO against another peripheral can place
;  one without reaching for an intrinsic, and so there is one place to
;  change if the barrier ever needs to be weaker.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure GpioBarrier()
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GpioReadReg(addr) - one 32-bit register, as a positive number.
;
;  PeekL sign-extends, so a register with bit 31 set arrives negative
;  and every subsequent shift and comparison is then wrong in a way that
;  only shows up on GPIO15, 31 or 47. The mask is not decoration.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i GpioReadReg(addr.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(addr) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GpioPinValid(pin) - 1 if the pin exists, 0 otherwise (and counted).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i GpioPinValid(pin.i)
  If pin < 0 Or pin > #PIN_MAX
    gpio_bad_pins = gpio_bad_pins + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The address arithmetic, one procedure per register family.
;
;  There is no modulo operator in this language, so every "pin mod N" is
;  written as pin - (pin/N)*N. Integer division truncates toward zero
;  and pin is non-negative at every call site (GpioPinValid ran first),
;  so that identity is exact here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; GPFSEL: ten 3-bit fields per register, six registers covering 0..59.
; Table 64 shows FSEL0..FSEL9 in GPFSEL0 at bits 2:0, 5:3, ... 29:27,
; with 31:30 Reserved - hence ten per register and a 3-bit stride.
Procedure.i GpioFselAddr(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn #GPIO_BASE + #GPFSEL0_OFF + (pin / 10) * 4
EndProcedure

Procedure.i GpioFselShift(pin.i)
  Define reg.i
  reg = pin / 10
  ProcedureReturn (pin - reg * 10) * 3
EndProcedure

; GPSET / GPCLR / GPLEV: one bit per pin, 32 pins per register, so bank
; 0 is pins 0..31 and bank 1 is pins 32..57 (Tables 70-75, pp.70-71;
; bank 1 has bits 31:26 Reserved, which is why #PIN_MAX is 57).
Procedure.i GpioBankAddr(base.i, pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn base + (pin / 32) * 4
EndProcedure

Procedure.i GpioBankMask(pin.i)
  Define bank.i
  bank = pin / 32
  ProcedureReturn 1 << (pin - bank * 32)
EndProcedure

; GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL: sixteen 2-bit fields per register, four registers
; covering 0..63 (Table 63 for the four offsets, Table 90 for the field
; layout - CNTRL00 at bits 1:0 up to CNTRL15 at bits 31:30).
Procedure.i GpioPullAddr(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn #GPIO_BASE + #GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_OFF + (pin / 16) * 4
EndProcedure

Procedure.i GpioPullShift(pin.i)
  Define reg.i
  reg = pin / 16
  ProcedureReturn (pin - reg * 16) * 2
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinMode(pin, mode) - function select.
;
;  mode is one of #PIN_INPUT, #PIN_OUTPUT, #PIN_ALT0..#PIN_ALT5 - i.e. a
;  raw 3-bit FSEL code. Returns 1 on success, 0 for a bad pin or a mode
;  outside 0..7.
;
;  READ-MODIFY-WRITE, and it has to be: one GPFSEL register carries ten
;  pins, so a blind store would set nine other pins to input. There is
;  no atomic set/clear alias for GPFSEL the way there is for the output
;  registers - Table 63 offers none - so this sequence is not
;  interrupt-safe or core-safe against another writer of the same
;  GPFSEL register. If a second core configures pins in the same group
;  of ten, that is the caller's problem to serialise; this library does
;  not own a lock.
;
;  NOTE THE ORDER WHEN YOU ARE SETTING UP AN INPUT: configure the pull
;  FIRST with PinPull(), then the mode. The other order works too - the
;  pull register is independent of FSEL - but doing the pull first means
;  the pin is never briefly an input with the previous pull on it.
;
;  Parentheses around every shift are not style. Operator precedence in
;  this language is NOT the host language's: & | ! bind TIGHTER than << >>, so
;  `v & ~7 << sh` would parse as `v & ~(7 << (sh))`... and worse forms
;  parse as something plausible. Written out, always.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinMode(pin.i, mode.i)
  Define a.i
  Define sh.i
  Define v.i

  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If mode < 0 Or mode > 7
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  GpioBarrier()                       ; section 1.3: before the first write
  a  = GpioFselAddr(pin)
  sh = GpioFselShift(pin)
  v  = GpioReadReg(a)
  v  = v & ~(7 << sh)
  v  = v | (mode << sh)
  PokeL(a, v)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinModeGet(pin) - the pin's current 3-bit FSEL code, or -1 for a bad
;  pin. Compare against #PIN_INPUT, #PIN_OUTPUT, #PIN_ALT0 and friends.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinModeGet(pin.i)
  Define v.i
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  v = (GpioReadReg(GpioFselAddr(pin)) >> GpioFselShift(pin)) & 7
  GpioBarrier()                       ; section 1.3: after the last read
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The three common cases, spelled out. PinAlt() takes 0..5 and does the
;  out-of-order mapping from Table 64 in ONE place, so no caller ever
;  has to remember that ALT4 is 3.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinInput(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn PinMode(pin, #PIN_INPUT)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PinOutput(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn PinMode(pin, #PIN_OUTPUT)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PinAlt(pin.i, alt.i)
  Define code.i
  Select alt
    Case 0
      code = #PIN_ALT0
    Case 1
      code = #PIN_ALT1
    Case 2
      code = #PIN_ALT2
    Case 3
      code = #PIN_ALT3
    Case 4
      code = #PIN_ALT4
    Case 5
      code = #PIN_ALT5
    Default
      ProcedureReturn 0
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn PinMode(pin, code)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinHigh / PinLow - the atomic set and clear registers.
;
;  Tables 70-73, p.70. These are WRITE-ONLY and writing a 0 to a bit
;  HAS NO EFFECT, which is the whole point: "Separating the set and
;  clear functions removes the need for read-modify-write operations".
;  So a single store touches exactly the one pin named and no other, and
;  two cores driving two different pins in the same bank cannot lose
;  each other's writes.
;
;  DO NOT try to read GPSET or GPCLR back. They are WO in the table; a
;  read returns nothing useful. The pin's actual state is GPLEV, which
;  is what DigitalRead() uses.
;
;  If the pin is currently an input the write is remembered but not
;  driven - Table 70's description: "if the pin is subsequently defined
;  as an output then the bit will be set according to the last set/clear
;  operation". That is a feature; it lets you stage a level before
;  switching the pin to output, so the pad never glitches through the
;  old value.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinHigh(pin.i)
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  PokeL(GpioBankAddr(#GPIO_BASE + #GPSET0_OFF, pin), GpioBankMask(pin))
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i PinLow(pin.i)
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  PokeL(GpioBankAddr(#GPIO_BASE + #GPCLR0_OFF, pin), GpioBankMask(pin))
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DigitalWrite(pin, level) - level 0 clears, anything else sets.
;
;  "Anything else sets" rather than "1 sets" so that DigitalWrite(pin,
;  DigitalRead(other)) and DigitalWrite(pin, flags & mask) both do the
;  obvious thing instead of quietly clearing when the mask value is 4.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DigitalWrite(pin.i, level.i)
  If level = #PIN_LOW
    ProcedureReturn PinLow(pin)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PinHigh(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DigitalRead(pin) - 0 or 1 from GPLEV, or -1 for a bad pin.
;
;  Tables 74-75, p.71: "The pin level registers return the actual value
;  of the pin." THE ACTUAL PAD, not a shadow of what you last wrote - so
;  this reads back a pin driven by something else, and it reads back an
;  output pin's real level, which is how you notice you are fighting an
;  external driver.
;
;  -1 for a bad pin, not 0. A bad pin returning 0 is indistinguishable
;  from a real low, which is exactly the silent wrong answer this
;  project keeps paying for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DigitalRead(pin.i)
  Define v.i
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  v = GpioReadReg(GpioBankAddr(#GPIO_BASE + #GPLEV0_OFF, pin))
  GpioBarrier()                       ; section 1.3: after the last read
  If (v & GpioBankMask(pin)) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #PIN_HIGH
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #PIN_LOW
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinToggle(pin) - drive the pin to the opposite of what the PAD reads.
;
;  There is no toggle register in Table 63, so this is read-then-write
;  and it is NOT atomic: another core, or an interrupt, can change the
;  pin between the GPLEV read and the GPSET/GPCLR write.
;
;  It also reads the PAD, not an intention. On an output pin that is the
;  same thing. On a pin being fought by an external driver it is not,
;  and PinToggle will keep re-deciding based on who is winning. If you
;  need a deterministic square wave, keep your own state and call
;  PinHigh/PinLow - a single write each, atomic by construction.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinToggle(pin.i)
  Define lvl.i
  lvl = DigitalRead(pin)
  If lvl < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If lvl = #PIN_HIGH
    ProcedureReturn PinLow(pin)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PinHigh(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinPull(pin, pull) - the BCM2711 pull-up / pull-down control.
;
;  pull is #PULL_NONE, #PULL_UP or #PULL_DOWN. Returns 1 on success, 0
;  for a bad pin or a value outside those three.
;
;  ***THIS IS THE BCM2711 MECHANISM, NOT THE BCM2835 ONE.*** No GPPUD,
;  no GPPUDCLK, no 150-cycle waits, no clocking sequence - a plain
;  read-modify-write of two bits in GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REGn. See the
;  long note in the file header for why a ported Pi 3 routine fails
;  here without complaining.
;
;  %11 IS REFUSED rather than written. Table 90 marks it Reserved, and
;  "reserved" on a pad control is not a value to find out about on a
;  board somebody else is using.
;
;  Read-modify-write, so the same caveat as PinMode: sixteen pins share
;  a register and this library does not serialise concurrent writers.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinPull(pin.i, pull.i)
  Define a.i
  Define sh.i
  Define v.i

  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If pull <> #PULL_NONE And pull <> #PULL_UP And pull <> #PULL_DOWN
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  GpioBarrier()
  a  = GpioPullAddr(pin)
  sh = GpioPullShift(pin)
  v  = GpioReadReg(a)
  v  = v & ~(3 << sh)
  v  = v | (pull << sh)
  PokeL(a, v)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PinPullGet(pin) - the pin's current pull state, or -1 for a bad pin.
;
;  A genuine read-back. Table 90's description: "Reading these registers
;  gives the current pull-state." Returns #PULL_NONE / #PULL_UP /
;  #PULL_DOWN, or 3 if the field somehow holds the reserved value.
;
;  Worth calling before you conclude a pin is floating: the reset states
;  are not uniform (Table 90 gives GPIO0-8 pull-up and GPIO9-15
;  pull-down) and the firmware sets its own pulls on pins it uses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PinPullGet(pin.i)
  Define v.i
  If GpioPinValid(pin) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  GpioBarrier()
  v = (GpioReadReg(GpioPullAddr(pin)) >> GpioPullShift(pin)) & 3
  GpioBarrier()                       ; section 1.3: after the last read
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GpioBadPinCount() - how many calls have been refused for a pin number
;  outside 0..#PIN_MAX since the program started.
;
;  Non-zero means somewhere a pin number is wrong, and the call it came
;  from did nothing. Cheaper to print this once at the end of a test
;  than to check a return code at four hundred call sites.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i GpioBadPinCount()
  ProcedureReturn gpio_bad_pins
EndProcedure
